				<article id="post-1120" class="entry content-bg single-entry post-1120 post type-post status-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail hentry category-closet-wardrobe category-small-spaces">
	<div class="entry-content-wrap">
		
<div class="entry-content single-content">
	
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best hat storage ideas do two jobs at once: they keep your hats easy to grab, and they protect their shape. A baseball cap can live happily on a peg, but a felt fedora or a wide-brim straw hat needs more care. Put them all in one pile on a chair and the brims bend, the crowns dent, and you can never find the one you want.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This guide covers 18 ideas for hat storage, from baseball caps and beanies to felt and straw hats, with options for closets, entryways, bedrooms and small apartments. Most are cheap, and many work for renters. If your closet is already packed, start with our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/closet-clean-out/">closet clean-out guide</a> so you only store the hats you actually wear.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-01-entryway-peg-rail.jpg" alt="Hat storage ideas: entryway wall with a wooden peg rail holding straw hats, a fedora and a beanie above a linen bench and woven basket" class="wp-image-1111" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-01-entryway-peg-rail.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-01-entryway-peg-rail-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-01-entryway-peg-rail-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-01-entryway-peg-rail-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group dsh-summary has-background" style="border-radius:12px;border-left-color:#8FA689;border-left-width:5px;background-color:#EEF3EC;padding-top:20px;padding-right:24px;padding-bottom:20px;padding-left:24px"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-0d2d72cd wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">


<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Key Takeaways</strong></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Hang everyday caps on pegs or an over-the-door hanger, keep felt and straw hats in boxes or on wide hooks so the crown and brim stay supported, and toss beanies in an open basket by the door.</li>



<li>Store hats by type, not all together. Caps can hang or nest; structured hats need their shape protected.</li>



<li>Keep the hats you wear this season within reach and move the rest to a high shelf or a box.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Time:</strong> 1–2 hours · <strong>Cost:</strong> $0–$80 · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Easy</p>


</div></div>


