elt Hats and Fedoras</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#6-store-your-best-hats-in-hat-boxes">6. Store your best hats in hat boxes</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#7-use-clear-stackable-hat-cases">7. Use clear, stackable hat cases</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#8-rest-felt-hats-upside-down-on-the-crown">8. Rest felt hats upside down on the crown</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#9-give-the-top-closet-shelf-to-hats">9. Give the top closet shelf to hats</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#straw-and-widebrim-hat-storage">Straw and Wide-Brim Hat Storage</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#10-turn-straw-hats-into-wall-decor">10. Turn straw hats into wall decor</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#11-give-each-widebrim-hat-its-own-hook">11. Give each wide-brim hat its own hook</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#12-keep-straw-hats-out-of-humid-rooms">12. Keep straw hats out of humid rooms</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#everyday-hats-by-the-door">Everyday Hats by the Door</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#13-add-a-peg-rail-by-the-front-door">13. Add a peg rail by the front door</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#14-beanie-storage-keep-a-basket-on-the-bench">14. Beanie storage: keep a basket on the bench</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#15-give-each-person-a-hook-or-a-bin">15. Give each person a hook or a bin</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#hat-storage-ideas-for-small-spaces-and-renters">Hat Storage Ideas for Small Spaces and Renters</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#16-use-removable-adhesive-hooks">16. Use removable adhesive hooks</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#17-store-offseason-hats-high-or-under-the-bed">17. Store off-season hats high or under the bed</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#18-edit-your-hats-once-a-season">18. Edit your hats once a season</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#common-mistakes-to-avoid">Common Mistakes to Avoid</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#faq">FAQ</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#what-is-the-best-way-to-store-hats">What is the best way to store hats?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-do-you-store-hats-without-ruining-them">How do you store hats without ruining them?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-do-you-store-a-lot-of-baseball-caps-in-a-small-space">How do you store a lot of baseball caps in a small space?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#can-you-hang-a-felt-hat-on-a-hook">Can you hang a felt hat on a hook?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#what-are-the-best-beanie-storage-ideas">What are the best beanie storage ideas?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-do-you-store-straw-hats-so-they-dont-crack">How do you store straw hats so they don&#039;t crack?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-do-you-store-hats-in-a-closet">How do you store hats in a closet?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-do-you-store-cowboy-hats">How do you store cowboy hats?</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#final-thoughts">Final Thoughts</a></li></ul></div></nav>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Match the Storage to the Hat</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Most hat storage problems come from treating every hat the same. A cotton cap is soft and forgiving. A felt hat holds its shape only as long as nothing presses on it. Straw dries out and cracks, and wool knits attract moths when they&#8217;re stored dirty. So before you buy anything, sort your hats into four groups and give each one the storage it needs.</p>



<figure class="dsh-diagram" style="margin:2em 0;"><svg viewBox="0 0 1200 900" width="100%" role="img" aria-labelledby="dshat-t dshat-d" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="font-family:Poppins,'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;height:auto;display:block;border-radius:12px;"><title id="dshat-t">How to store four types of hats</title><desc id="dshat-d">Four hat types and how to store each. Baseball caps: best on pegs, an over-the-door hanger or nested in a drawer by shape; avoid folding or crushing the brim. Felt hats and fedoras: best in a hat box with tissue in the crown, or resting upside down on the crown; avoid stacking anything on top. Straw and wide-brim hats: best on wall pegs or wide hooks on a wall without direct sun; avoid damp spots and strong sun. Knit beanies: best in an open basket or bin, washed before summer storage; avoid packing away dirty wool. Tip: support the crown and the brim, pad crowns with acid-free tissue and keep stored hats in the dark, based on the Canadian Conservation Institute.</desc><rect width="1200" height="900" fill="#F7F1E9"/><text x="60" y="84" font-size="44" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Match the Storage to the Hat</text><text x="60" y="124" font-size="24" fill="#6E645C">four hat types, four different needs</text><text x="1140" y="84" font-size="20" fill="#C85A32" text-anchor="end">dailyspacehacks.com</text><rect x="60" y="160" width="520" height="266" rx="16" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="60" y="160" width="520" height="8" rx="4" fill="#8FA689"/><circle cx="180" cy="302" r="100" fill="#DCE5D6" opacity="0.6"/><path d="M122 330 A58 52 0 0 1 238 330 Z" fill="#8FA689" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M200 330 L238 330 Q278 330 284 344 L200 344 Z" fill="#8FA689" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="180" cy="278" r="6" fill="#342E2A"/><path d="M180 284 V330" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.5"/><text x="300" y="218" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A