#342E2A" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.4"/><line x1="774" y1="608" x2="774" y2="630" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.4"/><line x1="786" y1="608" x2="786" y2="630" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.4"/><line x1="798" y1="608" x2="798" y2="630" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.4"/><text x="860" y="508" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Knit beanies</text><text x="860" y="550" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="#8FA689">BEST</text><text x="860" y="576" font-size="17" fill="#342E2A">An open basket or bin; wash</text><text x="860" y="600" font-size="17" fill="#342E2A">them before summer storage</text><text x="860" y="650" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="#C85A32">AVOID</text><text x="860" y="676" font-size="17" fill="#342E2A">Packing away dirty wool</text><rect x="60" y="750" width="1080" height="96" rx="14" fill="#8FA689"/><text x="600" y="790" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">Tip: support the crown and the brim</text><text x="600" y="822" font-size="17" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">Pad crowns with acid-free tissue, keep hats in the dark when stored, and never pile weight on top.</text><text x="600" y="878" font-size="15" fill="#6E645C" text-anchor="middle">Storage guidance based on the Canadian Conservation Institute, CCI Notes 13/12: Storage for Costume Accessories.</text></svg><figcaption style="text-align:center;font-size:0.9em;color:#6E645C;">Each hat type needs a different kind of support. Caps can hang or nest; felt and straw hats need their shape protected.</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This comes from how museums care for hats. The <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/conservation-institute/services/conservation-preservation-publications/canadian-conservation-institute-notes/storage-costume-accessories.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Canadian Conservation Institute</a> advises that hats be fully supported, crowns and brims included, padded with acid-free tissue, kept in the dark, and stored at a relative humidity of about 45 to 55%. You don&#8217;t need museum standards for a $15 cap, but the same ideas will keep a good felt or straw hat in shape for years.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Quick Pick: Hat Storage by Type</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Your hats</th><th>Best ideas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lots of baseball caps</td><td>Wall peg rack (#1), over-the-door hanger (#2), drawer (#3)</td></tr><tr><td>Felt hats and fedoras</td><td>Hat boxes (#6), clear hat cases (#7), crown-down on a shelf (#8)</td></tr><tr><td>Straw and wide-brim hats</td><td>Wall display (#10), one hook per hat (#11)</td></tr><tr><td>Beanies and winter hats</td><td>Basket by the door (#14), one bin per person (#15)</td></tr><tr><td>Small space or renting</td><td>Door hanger (#2), removable hooks (#16), under-bed box (#17)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Set Up Hat Storage</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Gather every hat</strong> from closets, hooks, cars and bags, so you know how many you really own.</li>



<li><strong>Edit.</strong> Let go of hats that are stretched, stained or never worn.</li>



<li><strong>Sort by type:</strong> caps, felt hats, straw hats and knits.</li>



<li><strong>Split everyday from occasional.</strong> Everyday hats go near the door; the rest go to the closet.</li>



<li><strong>Measure before you buy.</strong> Check the widest brim you own and the space you have, then pick racks, hooks or boxes that fit.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">18 Hat Storage Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These ideas are grouped by hat type, so you can jump to the hats you own the most.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Baseball Hat Storage Ideas</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-02-wall-cap-rack.jpg" alt="Baseball hat storage idea: two rows of wooden pegs on a hallway wall holding ten caps above a woven bench" class="wp-image-1112" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-02-wall-cap-rack.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-02-wall-cap-rack-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-02-wall-cap-rack-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-02-wall-cap-rack-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Hang caps on a wall peg rack</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A simple wooden rack with two rows of pegs turns a cap collection into a tidy display. Hang each cap by its back strap so the bill stays flat. Space pegs about 8 inches (20 cm) apart so the caps don&#8217;t crush each other. It&#8217;s one of the easiest baseball hat storage ideas for a hallway, bedroom or teen&#8217;s room.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-03-over-door-cap-hanger.jpg" alt="Over-the-door strap hanger holding six baseball caps on a cream bedroom closet door" class="wp-image-1113" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-03-over-door-cap-hanger.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-03-over-door-cap-hanger-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-03-over-door-cap-hanger-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-03-over-door-cap-hanger-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Use an over-the-door cap hanger</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An over-the-door strap with clips holds six to ten caps without any drilling, which makes it a good fit for renters and small bedrooms. Use it on a closet door so the caps stay out of sight. Check that the door still closes easily once it&#8217;s full. For more door tricks, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-closet-organization-ideas/">small closet organization ideas</a>.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-04-caps-in-drawer.jpg" alt="Open oak dresser drawer with a divider and baseball caps nested in two neat rows by color" class="wp-image-1114" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-04-caps-in-drawer.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-04-caps-in-drawer-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-04-caps-in-drawer-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-04-caps-in-drawer-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Nest caps in a dresser drawer</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Caps with the same shape nest neatly inside each other. Line them up front to back in a shallow drawer, with a divider down the middle, and sort them by color so you can see every one. Don&#8217;t fold structured caps; the crease in the front panel won&#8217;t come out.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Clip caps to a closet hanger</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A skirt hanger with clips, or a hanger with shower curtain rings looped over the bar, holds several caps on the closet rod. It uses hanging space you already have and keeps caps next to the clothes you wear them with.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Line up caps in a bin on a shelf</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you don&#8217;t wear caps often, nest them in a long, open bin on a closet shelf. Stand them on their sides like files so you can flip through them. A label on the front makes it easy to put them back.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Storage for Felt Hats and Fedoras</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-05-hat-boxes-closet-shelf.jpg" alt="Closet shelf with stacked linen hat boxes and clear hat cases holding felt hats above folded sweaters" class="wp-image-1115" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-05-hat-boxes-closet-shelf.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-05-hat-boxes-closet-shelf-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-05-hat-boxes-closet-shelf-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-05-hat-boxes-closet-shelf-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Store your best hats in hat boxes</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A round hat box is still the safest place for a felt hat you don&#8217;t wear every week. Choose one about 2 inches (5 cm) wider than the brim, stuff the crown with acid-free tissue, and keep nothing heavy on top. Linen or cardboard boxes look calm on an open shelf.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Use clear, stackable hat cases</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Clear hat cases protect against dust and let you see each hat without opening the lid. They stack two or three high on a closet shelf. Keep them away from a sunny window, though, since light fades felt and dyes.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Rest felt hats upside down on the crown</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If a fedora lives on a shelf, rest it upside down on its crown so the brim doesn&#8217;t carry the weight and flatten out. Never stack one felt hat inside another unless they&#8217;re the same size and shape.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Give the top closet shelf to hats</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The top shelf of a closet is perfect for hats you wear a few times a year. It&#8217;s dry, dark and out of the way. Put boxes and cases there and keep the heaviest items on lower shelves. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-walk-in-closet-ideas/">small walk-in closet ideas</a> show how to plan the shelves above the rod.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Straw and Wide-Brim Hat Storage</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-06-straw-hat-wall-display.jpg" alt="Bedroom wall above an oak dresser with a gallery of wide-brim straw hats hung on wooden pegs" class="wp-image-1116" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-06-straw-hat-wall-display.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-06-straw-hat-wall-display-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-06-straw-hat-wall-display-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-06-straw-hat-wall-display-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Turn straw hats into wall decor</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A wall of straw sun hats over a dresser or bed adds texture and keeps the hats ready for summer. Hang each one on a small wooden peg or a pin through the inside band, and mix sizes for a relaxed look. Choose a wall that doesn&#8217;t get direct afternoon sun.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Give each wide-brim hat its own hook</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Wide brims bend when they overlap. Space hooks about 16 to 18 inches (40 to 45 cm) apart, and hang the hats by the inner band or flat against the wall. If space is tight, hang them in a staggered pattern so the brims don&#8217;t touch.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Keep straw hats out of humid rooms</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Straw goes limp and can grow mildew in steamy rooms, and it turns brittle in hot, dry spots like an attic. Skip the bathroom and laundry room, and store straw hats in a bedroom or closet instead.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Everyday Hats by the Door</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Add a peg rail by the front door</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A long peg rail holds the hats you grab every day, along with bags and scarves. Add a shelf above it for one or two good hats. It&#8217;s an easy upgrade for any <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-entryway-ideas/">small entryway</a> because it keeps the floor clear.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-07-beanie-basket-bench.jpg" alt="Woven basket full of knit beanies on an oak entryway bench next to scarves, a plaid throw and boots" class="wp-image-1117" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-07-beanie-basket-bench.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-07-beanie-basket-bench-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-07-beanie-basket-bench-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/hat-storage-07-beanie-basket-bench-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Beanie storage: keep a basket on the bench</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Knit hats don&#8217;t need careful storage. Toss them in an open basket on an entryway bench or a cubby so kids can grab one and go. In spring, wash them before you store them for the summer, since clothes moths go after soiled wool.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Give each person a hook or a bin</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In a busy home, one hook or one small bin per person ends the morning hat hunt. Label them with names or colors. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/mudroom-ideas/">mudroom ideas</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/coat-closet-organization/">coat closet organization tips</a> show how to fit everyone&#8217;s things into a small drop zone.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Hat Storage Ideas for Small Spaces and Renters</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">16. Use removable adhesive hooks</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Removable hooks hold caps and light sun hats without drilling. Check the weight limit on the package and press them onto clean, smooth paint. They work well inside closet doors and on the side of a wardrobe.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">17. Store off-season hats high or under the bed</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Winter hats in summer and sun hats in winter don&#8217;t need prime space. Pack them in a box on a high shelf or in a flat under-bed bin, with tissue in the crowns of structured hats. Find more spots like these in our guide to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/storage-ideas-for-small-spaces/">storage ideas for small spaces</a>.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">18. Edit your hats once a season</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When you swap your seasonal hats, take a minute to let go of anything you didn&#8217;t wear. Fewer hats means every hat storage idea above works better, and you won&#8217;t need to buy more racks.</p>



